1. The "Image" Structure (The Manifest)

A P2P image shouldn't just be a folder; it needs a Manifest so the "puller" knows what they are getting before they download 2GB of mods.

JSON
{
  "name": "Ultra-Modded-Survival",
  "version": "1.20.1",
  "total_size_mb": 1450,
  "hash": "sha256-abc123...", 
  "required_java": "21",
  "layers": [
    {"name": "forge-engine", "hash": "..."},
    {"name": "mods-folder", "hash": "..."},
    {"name": "config-presets", "hash": "..."}
  ]
}
2. The Rust "Packer" (Compression)

In your Rust backend, use the tar and zstd crates. Zstd is significantly faster than Gzip, which is crucial for moving large Minecraft modpacks over a residential connection (like Flow in SVG).

Rust
use tar::Builder;
use zstd::stream::write::Encoder;

pub fn create_beacon_package(src_dir: &Path, output_file: &Path) -> std::io::Result<()> {
    let file = fs::File::create(output_file)?;
    // Level 3 compression is the "sweet spot" for speed vs size
    let mut encoder = Encoder::new(file, 3)?; 
    let mut archive = Builder::new(encoder);

    archive.append_dir_all(".", src_dir)?;
    archive.finish()?;
    Ok(())
}
3. The Transfer Logic (P2P via Cloudflare)

Since you're already using cloudflared, you can expose a local "Registry Port" on the host machine.

Host: Starts a tiny Axum (Rust) web server on 127.0.0.1:5000.

Host: cloudflared tunnels that port to a public URL (e.g., registry-user123.trycloudflare.com).

Peer: Your Electron app sends a GET request to that URL.

Rust Backend: Streams the .tar.zst file directly from the disk to the network.

4. Handling the "Missing Pieces"

When a peer "pulls" an image, your Rust code should perform a Differential Sync:

Check Local: "Do I already have Minecraft-1.20.1.jar?"

Skip: If yes, don't download it from the peer.

Fetch: Only download the unique mods and configs.

5. Why this is "Elite"

No Server Costs: You don't have to pay for S3 buckets or Docker Registry hosting.

Modpack Portability: Users can share their "Golden Images" (perfectly configured servers) instantly.

Privacy: Data stays between friends.

The "Beacon" Workflow:

User A clicks "Share Server" in Electron.

Rust zips the folder and starts a temporary Cloudflare tunnel.

User A sends a "Beacon Link" to User B.

User B pastes the link; their Rust backend pulls the manifest, validates the Java version, and starts the stream.

